(a) Alleged violations of this code of ethics must be submitted via written, sworn complaint to the clerk of the board of commissioners of the county. Consistent with the open meetings laws, the board of commissioners shall review all complaints against covered officials and employees except those complaints filed against a member of the board of commissioners. A majority of the full board of commissioners must agree that a sufficient basis has been given to warrant a public hearing. Certain violations alleged against employees may be referred through the proper channels within the Butts County Employee Personnel Policy's disciplinary procedures.
(b) The board of commissioners shall conduct the public hearing to determine whether, based upon clear and convincing evidence, the covered official or employee violated the code of ethics. Covered officials and employees are subject to the following penalties and action for violations of this code of ethics:
(1) Written reprimand or public censure;
(2) Recovery of value transferred or received by the county;
(3) Cancellation of the contract or rejection of the bid or offer;
(4) A monetary fine not to exceed $1,000.00; and
(5) Demotion or termination.
(c) If a complaint is received by the clerk of the board of commissioners which alleges a violation of the code of ethics by a member of the board of commissioners, an independent review board will be formed if the complaint alleges sufficient facts which, if proven to be true, would be a violation of the code of ethics. The county attorney of Lamar County, Monroe County, Spalding County, Newton County, Henry County and Jasper County, or any senior superior court judge within the State of Georgia, shall make the determination of whether or not a review board should be empaneled. If a review board is needed, the board shall consist of three persons who shall be county attorneys within reasonably close geographical proximity to Butts County or any senior superior court judge of the state. The County Attorney for Butts County is charged with the authority to contact such county attorney(s) or senior judge(s) as he deems proper to serve as the initial determination officer or review board member. The review board shall conduct a public hearing based upon the complaint and shall determine the existence of a violation by a majority vote, based on clear and convincing evidence. If a violation is found to exist, the review board shall recommend the penalty, which shall become the penalty imposed of the board of commissioners at the conclusion of the next regular meeting unless overturned or modified by a majority vote of the board of commissioners, excluding the board member subject to such penalty.
(Ord. of 9-04-2007, § I)

